WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.934 --> 00:00:02.201
-Our first guest tonight is

00:00:02.201 --> 00:00:04.436
an Academy Award-nominated
actress and entrepreneur

00:00:04.436 --> 00:00:07.072
you know from her work
in films like "Almost Famous,"

00:00:07.072 --> 00:00:08.507
"Mona Lisa and the Blood Moon,"

00:00:08.507 --> 00:00:10.076
and "How to Lose a Guy
in 10 Days."

00:00:10.076 --> 00:00:13.079
She stars In "Glass Onion:
A Knives Out Mystery,"

00:00:13.079 --> 00:00:15.281
which premieres on Netflix,
December 23rd.

00:00:15.281 --> 00:00:17.483
Please welcome back to the show
our friend Kate Hudson,

00:00:17.483 --> 00:00:18.618
everybody.

00:00:18.618 --> 00:00:20.886
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:20.886 --> 00:00:23.189
[ Mid-tempo music plays ]

00:00:23.189 --> 00:00:28.694
♪♪

00:00:28.694 --> 00:00:34.200
♪♪

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:36.335
-Hi!

00:00:36.335 --> 00:00:38.938
-It is so lovely
to have you here in person.

00:00:38.938 --> 00:00:41.073
-It's nice to be here
with an audience.

00:00:41.073 --> 00:00:42.942
-Yes, the last time you were
here in person,

00:00:42.942 --> 00:00:44.877
there was nobody in the seats.
-There was no audience.

00:00:44.877 --> 00:00:46.579
-So, thank you so much
for suffering through that,

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:48.614
and now your reward.
-I'm so happy.

00:00:48.614 --> 00:00:49.849
I love an audience.

00:00:49.849 --> 00:00:51.351
-You get a nice
Christmas audience.

00:00:51.351 --> 00:00:53.119
-I love that you do
a guest drummer,

00:00:53.119 --> 00:00:54.753
because my son Bing
is a drummer.

00:00:54.753 --> 00:00:57.423
Like, been drumming
since he was a year old.

00:00:57.423 --> 00:01:00.426
-And now, a year old, who --
did you guys get him a drum kit?

00:01:00.426 --> 00:01:02.028
Because I feel like
that's a very cruel gift

00:01:02.028 --> 00:01:04.664
to give another person's child.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:04.664 --> 00:01:07.199
-Yeah, we got him
the drum kit, but he --

00:01:07.199 --> 00:01:09.202
-How do you know, at one,
that he wanted it?

00:01:09.202 --> 00:01:13.306
-Because he took -- So, his dad
is in a band called Muse.

00:01:13.306 --> 00:01:17.176
And Dom Howard, the drummer,
had to drumstick.

00:01:17.176 --> 00:01:19.312
He couldn't even walk.
He took the drumsticks,

00:01:19.312 --> 00:01:21.714
and that was it.
They've never left his hands.

00:01:21.714 --> 00:01:24.484
-Really?
-He's 11 years old. He's insane.

00:01:24.484 --> 00:01:26.519
Can he be a guest drummer
one day?

00:01:26.519 --> 00:01:28.087
-I would love to have that.

00:01:28.087 --> 00:01:30.489
We are gonna have to have him
submit a demo tape.

00:01:30.489 --> 00:01:32.058
-Okay, okay.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:32.058 --> 00:01:34.026
-But, this is very --
So, again, obviously,

00:01:34.026 --> 00:01:36.496
you know, he's got music
in his bloodline.

00:01:36.496 --> 00:01:38.464
But could you -- Did you guys
have an ear for it right away?

00:01:38.464 --> 00:01:40.166
Were you were like, "Oh, he's
actually maybe gonna have

00:01:40.166 --> 00:01:41.167
some talent?"
Like, at what point --

00:01:41.167 --> 00:01:42.435
Be honest now --
don't be like,

00:01:42.435 --> 00:01:44.537
"At one, we could tell."
-It was like 18 months.

00:01:44.537 --> 00:01:47.139
He would, like, sit
and he would --

00:01:47.139 --> 00:01:50.109
he would put pillows and
he'd do different heights.

00:01:50.109 --> 00:01:52.712
And it was like --
[ Mimicking drumming ]

00:01:52.712 --> 00:01:53.946
And we're like,
"What's happening?"

00:01:53.946 --> 00:01:55.948
-I mean, if you got him
to play drums on pillows,

00:01:55.948 --> 00:01:59.418
you're [Bleep] geniuses.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:59.418 --> 00:02:01.721
-It was crazy.
-That's very impressive.

00:02:01.721 --> 00:02:05.524
-And now, just recently, because
he's the kind of kid who goes,

00:02:05.524 --> 00:02:07.861
"Mom, I'm gonna go play drums"
four times a day.

00:02:07.861 --> 00:02:09.328
So, he goes --
And I had to, like --

00:02:09.328 --> 00:02:11.029
You know, when you have a kid
like that,

00:02:11.029 --> 00:02:13.800
it's, like, you want to
support that as much as you can.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:17.303
And so the pool house,
which is more like a shed,

00:02:17.303 --> 00:02:19.872
became the drum room.
And so that's Bing's room,

00:02:19.872 --> 00:02:21.941
which all the other siblings
are jealous

00:02:21.941 --> 00:02:23.376
because he has his own room.
-Yeah.

00:02:23.376 --> 00:02:25.878
-And then Ryder plays guitar --
his older brother.

00:02:25.878 --> 00:02:28.314
And Bing just decided --
Ryder went to college.

00:02:28.314 --> 00:02:30.116
Bing just decided,
"Well, you know what?

00:02:30.116 --> 00:02:31.383
I got to pick up the guitar."

00:02:31.383 --> 00:02:34.020
He's just, like, shredding
in like two months.

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:37.523
And Ryder's like, "God."
[ Laughter ]

00:02:37.523 --> 00:02:40.426
Taken Ryder like two years
to learn how to play the guitar.

00:02:40.426 --> 00:02:42.895
Bing, in, like, a month.
-That's amazing.

00:02:42.895 --> 00:02:44.531
You're very lucky
when you know --

00:02:44.531 --> 00:02:46.032
when your kids know
what they want.

00:02:46.032 --> 00:02:47.666
It's a very sweet thing to know.
-Yeah, it's --

00:02:47.666 --> 00:02:49.768
-'Cause my oldest,
his thing is shoplifting,

00:02:49.768 --> 00:02:51.871
which has been such a drag.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:51.871 --> 00:02:53.439
He's great at it.
We never know

00:02:53.439 --> 00:02:55.241
till we get back in the car,
and then he's like...

00:02:55.241 --> 00:02:58.710
-Well, you can --You...
[ Laughter ]

00:02:58.710 --> 00:03:00.679
You could probably funnel that
into something fantastic.

00:03:00.679 --> 00:03:01.881
-I hope so.
We're really --

00:03:01.881 --> 00:03:04.150
you know, that's the goal.
So, your oldest --

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:05.751
[ Laughter ]

00:03:05.751 --> 00:03:08.221
I know you have three,
just like I do.

00:03:08.221 --> 00:03:09.856
And your oldest
was off to college.

00:03:09.856 --> 00:03:11.858
Do the younger ones
get very excited

00:03:11.858 --> 00:03:13.693
about the homecoming
at the holidays?

00:03:13.693 --> 00:03:16.629
-Oh, this Christmas,
it's, like -- it's a thing.

00:03:16.629 --> 00:03:18.864
I mean, it's, like, the fact
that Ryder's coming home.

00:03:18.864 --> 00:03:20.466
-It's his first time coming home
for Christmas, right?

00:03:20.466 --> 00:03:22.334
-It's his first time
coming home.

00:03:22.334 --> 00:03:26.639
And even the cousins.
'Cause we do big Christmases,

00:03:26.639 --> 00:03:29.074
since we're all together.
And everybody --

00:03:29.074 --> 00:03:31.977
I mean, Ryder coming home,
is, like, the big...

00:03:31.977 --> 00:03:33.579
-That's really exciting.
-Me, as well,

00:03:33.579 --> 00:03:35.748
but I don't want to,
like, be that mom.

00:03:35.748 --> 00:03:37.216
-[ Laughs ]
-You know?

00:03:37.216 --> 00:03:38.517
-Oh, you can't -- Come on,
you got to be that mom.

00:03:38.517 --> 00:03:40.520
Who are you hiding it from?
They know you're excited.

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:42.888
-I know.
-You can't play hard to get

00:03:42.888 --> 00:03:44.991
when you're a mom.
Like, "Oh, you? Whatever."

00:03:44.991 --> 00:03:47.126
[ Laughter ]

00:03:47.126 --> 00:03:48.894
-Yeah, that's true. That's true.

00:03:48.894 --> 00:03:52.131
-Your mom -- this is very
sweet -- came to the premiere.

00:03:52.131 --> 00:03:53.532
Here's Goldie Hawn.
-Yeah.

00:03:53.532 --> 00:03:55.435
-Came to the premiere
of "Knives Out."

00:03:55.435 --> 00:03:58.738
-Aww, Mommy.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:58.738 --> 00:04:02.374
-Do you feel any different
watching your work

00:04:02.374 --> 00:04:07.646
with a parent around?
-Uhh...

00:04:07.646 --> 00:04:09.315
-Like, when you're
at the premiere,

00:04:09.315 --> 00:04:11.317
are you kind of checking
to see if she's enjoying it?

00:04:11.317 --> 00:04:14.520
-Yeah, especially this movie,
because it's so comedic.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:17.256
And -- And, I knew
she would love it.

00:04:17.256 --> 00:04:21.594
She was far more excited
to see Edward after the movie

00:04:21.594 --> 00:04:23.196
than me.
I was like, "Mom, what --

00:04:23.196 --> 00:04:25.564
Mom? Mom?"
She's like, "Edward!"

00:04:25.564 --> 00:04:27.333
Like everybody, you know --
she was --

00:04:27.333 --> 00:04:29.535
But she loved it.
-That's good.

00:04:29.535 --> 00:04:31.503
-It's really --
I was, I was --

00:04:31.503 --> 00:04:33.305
It's a shame my dad
wasn't there,

00:04:33.305 --> 00:04:36.375
'cause Kurt has that laugh.
Like, I remember growing up

00:04:36.375 --> 00:04:37.977
and doing plays and everything.

00:04:37.977 --> 00:04:40.479
And he was always the loudest
in the room.

00:04:40.479 --> 00:04:43.249
And I know, with "Glass Onion",
'cause it's just

00:04:43.249 --> 00:04:45.518
such a funny movie,
that in that theater,

00:04:45.518 --> 00:04:48.087
he would have -- I mean,
he would have been loud and --

00:04:48.087 --> 00:04:49.721
-What do they do
when they don't like it?

00:04:49.721 --> 00:04:51.857
Can you tell?
-Oh, yeah.

00:04:51.857 --> 00:04:54.127
-Yeah.
-Oh, yeah, totally honest.

00:04:54.127 --> 00:04:56.095
Like, they're not --
My parents were

00:04:56.095 --> 00:04:57.563
the kind of parents
that, like, when we would

00:04:57.563 --> 00:05:01.901
put on a play at Christmastime,
they -- We...

00:05:01.901 --> 00:05:04.503
One time, we did one play,
and Kurt, at the end, goes,

00:05:04.503 --> 00:05:07.039
"I just don't get it."
[ Laughter ]

00:05:07.039 --> 00:05:09.174
We were like -- We were like 6.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:09.174 --> 00:05:11.844
And we're like,
"Uh, what do you mean?"

00:05:11.844 --> 00:05:13.946
He was like, "I don't get
what's happening.

00:05:13.946 --> 00:05:16.416
Like, what's your story?
Guys, there's no middle,

00:05:16.416 --> 00:05:17.850
there's no end.
Like, what is this?"

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:19.852
-You got studio notes
on a Christmas play.

00:05:19.852 --> 00:05:25.758
-He made us go back
and, like, rewrite our play

00:05:25.758 --> 00:05:30.062
and then re-come down
and perform it.

00:05:30.062 --> 00:05:33.332
And he had great --
I mean, look, great notes.

00:05:33.332 --> 00:05:35.868
-And by the way, he's had
success raising actors.

00:05:35.868 --> 00:05:37.370
So you got to give him credit.
-Yeah, I mean...

00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:39.204
You know,
it was always like that.

00:05:39.204 --> 00:05:41.206
I remember, one time,
he also said, you know,

00:05:41.206 --> 00:05:44.477
"Well, honey, there's always
your first movie."

00:05:44.477 --> 00:05:45.911
[ Laughter ]

00:05:45.911 --> 00:05:47.579
That was his retract
from my first film.

00:05:47.579 --> 00:05:49.215
-So, his review
of your first movie was

00:05:49.215 --> 00:05:50.716
that it was your first?
-"There's always your first."

00:05:50.716 --> 00:05:53.786
-Yeah, no one will ever say
it wasn't your first.

00:05:53.786 --> 00:05:55.254
[ Laughter ]
-That's right.

00:05:55.254 --> 00:05:56.822
-I have a lot more
to ask you about.

00:05:56.822 --> 00:05:59.092
We will be right back
with more from Kate Hudson.

